<DocsLayout title="Reasoning and FPF in Haft v9" description="Source-native FPF Query, independent skills, and the boundary between retrieval, application, authority, and work.">
  <h1>Reasoning &amp; FPF</h1>

  <p>
    Haft v9 does not impose a five-stage reasoning workflow. Start from the
    project object and the current question. Use the smallest capability that
    can return the needed result.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>h-reason</code> is the source-first umbrella for an ambiguous or
    cross-cutting question. If the need is already clear, use the exact skill:
    <code>h-frame</code>, <code>h-diagnose</code>, <code>h-explore</code>,
    <code>h-compare</code>, <code>h-decide</code>, <code>h-verify</code>,
    <code>h-status</code>, <code>h-onboard</code>, <code>h-spec</code>,
    <code>h-note</code>, or the manual-only <code>h-commission</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>When Haft queries FPF</h2>

  <p>
    Mechanical edits, exact project lookups, and status-only work do not need
    an FPF query. When pattern applicability is material or uncertain, the
    agent retrieves the pinned FPF publication before applying a pattern.
  </p>

  <pre><code>haft fpf query "How should I frame this concern?" --json
haft fpf lookup "E.11.PUA" --json
haft fpf inspect "E.11.PUA" --role pattern_body --json</code></pre>

  <p>The three commands have different return boundaries:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Command</th>
        <th>Result</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>query</code></td>
        <td>Compact source-derived candidates for a concern</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>lookup</code></td>
        <td>Exact identifier first; compact candidates when no exact unit resolves</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>inspect</code></td>
        <td>One exact source unit or an explicit abstention</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Every route returns the closed
    <code>exact_hit | candidate_set | abstained</code> union. Candidate order is
    retrieval output. It is not applicability, recommendation, precedence,
    authority, or work order.
  </p>

  <h2>Progressive disclosure</h2>

  <p>
    Concern retrieval starts from compact source roles such as practical-use
    cards and table-of-contents rows. After selecting a candidate by the
    current condition and required result, the agent recovers the full pattern
    body and reads its problem, forces, solution, boundaries, worked slices,
    and checklist.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use <code>--view working</code> for the bounded default response,
    <code>--view trace</code> for provenance and replay coordinates, and
    <code>--view diagnostic</code> for retrieval internals. These are views of
    one canonical result, not different reasoning methods.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Non-English concerns:</strong> keep the operator's original query.
      Add precise English or FPF terms through the current object's context
      fields; do not replace the request with a hidden translation or a
      Haft-owned route catalog.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Retrieval is not application</h2>

  <p>
    FPF Query returns source. The agent still has to compare relevant
    candidates, recover the exact full pattern, check its ordinary boundary,
    and decide whether it applies. When the basis is insufficient, it keeps
    several candidates live or abstains.
  </p>

  <p>
    A retrieved pattern does not approve a specification, bind a decision,
    authorize execution, prove a claim, or perform work. Those effects use
    separate records and authority boundaries.
  </p>

  <h2>Persistence is conditional</h2>

  <p>
    Ordinary bounded reasoning can remain in the conversation. Haft writes a
    project record when the operator explicitly asks to save or bind it, or
    when current work has a concrete receiving use such as handoff, replay,
    audit, automation, delayed evidence, or costly reversal.
  </p>

  <p>
    An empty graph or a failed memory lookup is not a reason to persist. When
    persistence is justified, Haft writes only the records needed by that
    receiving use.
  </p>

  <h2>Authority remains separate</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><code>h-decide</code> may route one direct, unambiguous operator request for one bounded DecisionRecord effect.</li>
    <li>A recommendation, quotation, schema, or tool argument is not an operator request.</li>
    <li><code>h-commission</code> stays manual-only because it grants bounded execution authority.</li>
    <li>A WorkPlan describes intended work; it does not perform or authorize that work.</li>
  </ul>

